Chapter – "speak" (Text-to-Speech)

What is "speak"?

"speak" is a built-in Tysc keyword that converts text into spoken audio using the computer's Text-to-Speech (TTS) engine.

Instead of displaying text on the screen like "vokar", "speak" reads the text aloud.

---

Why Use "speak"?

Use "speak" when your program needs to:

- Read messages aloud.
- Assist visually impaired users.
- Create voice assistants.
- Give spoken notifications.
- Build educational applications.

---

Syntax

speak expression

The "expression" can be:

- A string
- A variable
- A text expression
- The result of a function

---

Syntax Breakdown

Example:

speak "Welcome to Tysc"

"speak"

Keyword

Starts a Text-to-Speech operation.

The interpreter recognizes that the following expression should be spoken instead of printed.

---

""Welcome to Tysc""

A string literal.

This is the text that will be spoken.

---

How the Interpreter Works

When the interpreter reads:

speak "Welcome to Tysc"

it performs these steps:

1. Reads the "speak" keyword.
2. Evaluates the expression after "speak".
3. Converts the result into text (if necessary).
4. Sends the text to the operating system's Text-to-Speech engine.
5. The computer speaks the text aloud.
6. The program continues with the next statement.

---

Examples

Speak a String

speak "Hello, World!"

Speech:

Hello, World!

---

Speak a Variable

name = "Emil"

speak name

Speech:

Emil

---

Speak an Expression

name = "Emil"

speak "Welcome, " + name

Speech:

Welcome, Emil

---

Speak Inside a Loop

walk i from 1 to 3
    speak "Number " + i
End

Speech:

Number 1
Number 2
Number 3

---

Speak Inside a Function

fun welcome(name)
    speak "Welcome to Tysc, " + name
End

welcome("Emil")

Speech:

Welcome to Tysc, Emil

---

Difference Between "vokar" and "speak"

Keyword| Purpose
"vokar"| Displays text on the screen.
"speak"| Speaks text through the speaker.

Example:

vokar//"Hello"
speak "Hello"

- "vokar" prints Hello.
- "speak" says Hello aloud.

---

Common Mistakes

Speaking an Undefined Variable

speak username

If "username" has not been created, the interpreter should report an error.

---

No Audio Device

If the system has no Text-to-Speech engine or no audio output device, the interpreter should report a runtime error or fall back to displaying the text.

---

Best Practices

- Use "speak" for important spoken messages.
- Keep spoken text short and clear.
- Combine "vokar" and "speak" when users need both visual and audio feedback.
- Ensure a Text-to-Speech engine is available on the system.

---

Summary

"speak" is Tysc's built-in Text-to-Speech feature. It converts text into spoken audio, allowing programs to communicate with users through voice. It can speak strings, variables, and expressions, making it useful for accessibility, assistants, educational software, and voice-enabled applications.
